   "widgets_values": [
    "### H3 Rolling Timeline\n\nA long H3 video from pictures and words. **Shots** are rendered on their own. Each **bridge** is rendered between two finished shots and locked to the last 5 frames of one and to 5 frames of the next. Nothing is ever rendered from a bridge.\n\n1. Pick your models in the loaders on the left.\n2. Load pictures. `picture_1` is image 1, and so on. Unplug the ones you don't need.\n3. **prompt**: sent as typed to every piece. For a plain run that's all; `shots` says how many.\n4. **storyboard** (optional): `>` starts a shot, `~` starts a bridge, and what follows is that piece's text. It goes where `{beat}` is in the prompt, or on the end if there is no `{beat}`.\n\nSettings, lowercase, right after the marker:\n- `7s` or `158f`: length\n- `image 3` / `-image 2`: start / stop handing over that picture, from this piece on\n- `take 2`: another roll of this piece. `seed 5001`: that exact seed\n- `solo`: render only this piece (a bridge brings its two shots)\n\nAbove the first piece (optional): `image 1: <Subject 1> is ... <Picture>.` with `keep: ...` under it, and `start: image 1, image 2`. The node writes each piece's definition and retention lines from the pictures that piece has.\n\nThe boxes hold a worked example: a woman writes two lines on a chalkboard. Pictures 2, 3 and 4 are the same room with a blank board, one line, and both lines. The writing happens in the bridges; each bridge keeps the picture from before the change and the shot after it gets the new one.\n\nPieces already rendered come back from a cache, so changing one piece only re-renders it and the bridges that touch it. Long runs are written to `output/h3_rolling/`."
   ],
   "title": "How to"
  }
